=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company runs its own survey of customers to measure satisfaction with a new product. What type of data does it collect?
+ Primary data || It is collected specifically for this purpose.
- Secondary data || Secondary data was collected for another purpose.
- Continuous data only || The question concerns why it was collected, not its measurement.
- Census data || A census covers the whole population; that is not stated.
explain: Primary data is collected for the survey in question; secondary data already exists, collected for another purpose.
mistake: Thinking all survey data is secondary.
tip: Collected for this purpose = primary.
src: Study text ch2a - types of data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which of the following is DISCRETE data?
+ The number of complaints received each day || It can only take whole-number values.
- The weight of each bag of flour || Weight can take any value.
- The time taken to serve a customer || Time can take any value.
- The temperature in a warehouse || Temperature is continuous.
explain: Discrete data takes countable values; continuous data can take any value within a range.
mistake: Thinking measured quantities are discrete.
tip: Counted = discrete; measured = continuous.
src: Study text ch2a - discrete and continuous (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is a census?
+ A survey that examines every member of the population || It covers the whole population.
- A survey of a random sample || That is sampling.
- A list of all items in a population || That is a sampling frame.
- A survey of one cluster only || That is cluster sampling.
explain: A census is complete but usually expensive and slow; sampling is often more practical.
mistake: Confusing a census with a sampling frame.
tip: Census = everyone.
src: Study text ch2a - census (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which list gives the "three Vs" commonly used to describe Big Data?
+ Volume, velocity and variety || These describe the scale, speed and range of formats.
- Value, verification and validity || Not the three Vs listed.
- Variance, volatility and volume || Not the three Vs listed.
- Visibility, value and variety || Not the three Vs listed.
explain: Big Data involves huge volumes, created and processed at high speed, in many different formats.
mistake: Inventing extra Vs.
tip: How much, how fast, how varied.
src: Study text ch2a - Big Data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is a sampling frame?
+ A numbered list of all the items in a population || It is needed for random sampling.
- The sample that has been selected || That is the sample.
- The size of the sample || Not the meaning.
- A graph of the sample results || Not the meaning.
explain: Random sampling needs a sampling frame so each item can be identified and selected.
mistake: Confusing the frame with the sample.
tip: Frame = full numbered list.
src: Study text ch2a - sampling frame (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which of the following is an INTERNAL source of data for a business?
+ The payroll records || Records within the organisation are internal sources.
- Government statistics on inflation || External.
- A trade journal || External.
- A competitor's website || External.
explain: Internal sources include accounting records, records linked to the accounting system (payroll, production, sales) and staff knowledge.
mistake: Thinking any financial data is internal.
tip: Inside the business = internal.
src: Study text ch2a - sources (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: In which sampling method does every item in the population have an equal chance of selection?
+ Simple random sampling || Each item has the same chance.
- Quota sampling || Selection is left to interviewers.
- Cluster sampling || Only one cluster is surveyed.
- Convenience sampling || Not random.
explain: Simple random sampling needs a sampling frame and gives every item an equal chance.
mistake: Thinking quota sampling is random.
tip: Equal chance = simple random.
src: Study text ch2a - random sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which description fits SECONDARY data?
+ Data originally collected for some other purpose || It is reused for a new purpose.
- Data collected specifically for the current investigation || That is primary data.
- Data that is always inaccurate || Secondary data can be accurate.
- Data that is only qualitative || It can be quantitative.
explain: Secondary data is cheaper and quicker to obtain but may not fit the current purpose exactly.
mistake: Thinking secondary means less important.
tip: Secondary = second-hand.
src: Study text ch2a - secondary data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Which sampling method is NON-probability?
+ Quota sampling || The chance of each member being selected is not known.
- Simple random sampling || Probability method.
- Stratified random sampling || Probability method.
- Systematic sampling || Probability method.
explain: In probability sampling each member has a known chance of selection; quota sampling forfeits randomness.
mistake: Thinking quota sampling is stratified.
tip: Quota = interviewer chooses.
src: Study text ch2a - probability vs non-probability (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which is an example of QUALITATIVE data?
+ Customers' favourite colour of packaging || It is an attribute, not a measurement.
- The number of units sold || Quantitative.
- The cost of each order || Quantitative.
- The hours worked by staff || Quantitative.
explain: Quantitative data can be measured numerically; qualitative data describes attributes.
mistake: Thinking all business data is quantitative.
tip: Measured = quantitative; described = qualitative.
src: Study text ch2a - types of data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A population of 2,000 accountants includes 500 who work in partnerships. A stratified sample of 100 accountants is required. How many partnership accountants should be included?
answer: 25
tol: 0
unit: people
formula: Stratum sample = (stratum size / population) x sample size
step: Partnership share of population = 500 / 2,000 = 25%.
step: Sample from partnerships = 25% x 100 = 25.
check: 500/2000*100
explain: Stratified sampling takes a random sample from each stratum in proportion to its share of the population.
mistake: Taking an equal number from each stratum.
tip: Proportion in population = proportion in sample.
src: Study text ch2a - stratified sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A national survey selects 8 regions, then 5 towns in each region, then 200 people in each town. How many people are sampled in total?
answer: 8000
tol: 0
unit: people
formula: Total = regions x towns per region x people per town
step: Towns sampled = 8 x 5 = 40.
step: People sampled = 40 x 200 = 8,000.
check: 8*5*200
explain: Multistage sampling divides the population in stages, making large surveys practical.
mistake: Adding instead of multiplying.
tip: Multistage totals multiply.
src: Study text ch2a - multistage sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: mcq
level: S
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: A firm selects every 20th invoice from its numbered sales listing, starting from a randomly chosen number between 1 and 20. Which method is this?
+ Systematic sampling || Every nth item after a random start.
- Simple random sampling || Not every selection is independently random.
- Quota sampling || Not interviewer-selected.
- Cluster sampling || Not a single subsection.
explain: Systematic sampling is simple but can be biased if the list has a repeating pattern.
mistake: Calling it simple random.
tip: Every nth = systematic.
src: Study text ch2a - systematic sampling (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q014
type: mcq
level: S
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: A market researcher must interview 50 men and 50 women aged over 60 in a shopping centre and chooses whom to approach. Which method is this?
+ Quota sampling || A set number from each group, chosen by the interviewer.
- Stratified random sampling || Selection within groups would be random.
- Systematic sampling || Not every nth person.
- Census || Not the whole population.
explain: Quota sampling is cheap and simple but not random, and interviewers may introduce bias.
mistake: Confusing quota with stratified sampling.
tip: Interviewer chooses = quota.
src: Study text ch2a - quota sampling (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q015
type: mcq
level: S
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: A retailer wanting to survey customers chooses one town at random and surveys every customer in that town. Which method is this?
+ Cluster sampling || One subsection is chosen and all of it surveyed.
- Multistage sampling || Multistage involves several stages of selection.
- Quota sampling || Not interviewer quotas.
- Systematic sampling || Not every nth item.
explain: Cluster sampling is cheap but may be unrepresentative if the cluster differs from the population.
mistake: Calling it multistage.
tip: One whole cluster = cluster sampling.
src: Study text ch2a - cluster sampling (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q016
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is the defining feature of a probability sampling method?
+ Each member of the population has a known chance of being selected || Selection probabilities are known.
- The sample is chosen by the interviewer || That is quota sampling.
- Every member is surveyed || That is a census.
- The sample size is always 100 || Size is not the defining feature.
explain: Probability methods include simple random, stratified, systematic and multistage sampling.
mistake: Thinking probability means equal chance in every method.
tip: Known chance = probability sampling.
src: Study text ch2a - probability sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO are drawbacks of simple random sampling?
+ It needs a sampling frame, which may not exist || A full list is required.
+ The selected items may be widely spread, making data collection costly || Random items may be scattered geographically.
- Every item has an equal chance of selection || That is an advantage.
- It is non-probability sampling || It is a probability method.
explain: Random sampling may also, by chance, be unrepresentative of important groups.
mistake: Listing its main advantage as a drawback.
tip: Random is fair, but not always practical.
src: Study text ch2a - random sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which is a SECONDARY source of economic data?
+ Official government statistics || Collected by government for general use.
- A firm's own customer interviews || Primary data.
- The firm's sales ledger || Internal records, collected for accounting.
- A focus group the firm runs || Primary data.
explain: Main secondary sources include governments, banks, financial newspapers, trade journals, information bureaux, consultancies, libraries and the internet.
mistake: Confusing internal records with secondary sources.
tip: Published by others = secondary source.
src: Study text ch2a - secondary sources (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which BEST describes "velocity" in the context of Big Data?
+ The speed at which data is created and must be processed || Big Data often arrives in real time.
- The amount of data stored || That is volume.
- The range of data formats || That is variety.
- The accuracy of the data || Not one of the three Vs.
explain: High velocity means data streams, such as online transactions, need rapid processing.
mistake: Confusing velocity with volume.
tip: Velocity = speed.
src: Study text ch2a - three Vs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Match each sampling method with its description.
pair: Simple random => Every item in the population has an equal chance
pair: Stratified random => Random sample taken from each group in proportion
pair: Systematic => Every nth item after a random start
pair: Quota => Interviewers fill a set number from each group
pair: Cluster => One subsection is chosen and all of it surveyed
explain: Recognising each method lets you choose the right one for a situation.
mistake: Confusing stratified with quota.
tip: Random within groups = stratified; interviewer chooses = quota.
src: Study text ch2a - sampling methods (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which item is CONTINUOUS data?
+ The length of telephone calls in minutes and seconds || Time can take any value.
- The number of children in a family || Discrete.
- The number of cars sold || Discrete.
- Shoe sizes sold in whole and half sizes || These are fixed steps - discrete.
explain: Continuous data can take any value within a range, limited only by the precision of measurement.
mistake: Treating shoe sizes as continuous.
tip: Can it be measured more precisely? Then continuous.
src: Study text ch2a - continuous data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which is a common CRITICISM of Big Data analysis?
+ It may find correlations that are mistaken for cause and effect || Correlation is not causation.
- It always uses too little data || Big Data uses a great deal of data.
- It can only analyse financial data || It covers many formats.
- It is always slower than traditional analysis || It can be faster.
explain: Criticisms include confusing correlation with causation, sampling bias and overconfidence in large datasets.
mistake: Assuming more data guarantees better conclusions.
tip: Big data can still give wrong answers.
src: Study text ch2a - Big Data criticisms (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: When is systematic sampling MOST likely to give a biased sample?
+ When the population list has a repeating pattern that coincides with the sampling interval || Every nth item may always fall on the same type.
- When a random start is used || A random start is part of the method.
- When the population is large || Size alone does not cause bias.
- When a sampling frame exists || A frame is needed.
explain: For example, choosing every 7th day from a daily list would always pick the same weekday.
mistake: Assuming systematic sampling is always unbiased.
tip: Watch for patterns in the list.
src: Study text ch2a - systematic sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How can changes in interest rates in the economic environment affect a business?
+ They change the cost of borrowing and can affect customer demand || Economic factors affect costs and activity levels.
- They have no effect on businesses || They do affect businesses.
- They only affect banks || They affect all borrowers and spenders.
- They change accounting standards || Not related.
explain: The economic environment affects firms through activity levels, exchange rates, interest rates and inflation.
mistake: Ignoring economic data as an external source.
tip: Rates up - borrowing dearer, spending lower.
src: Study text ch2a - economic environment (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: An auditor wants a sample of invoices that represents each of three branches in proportion to their number of invoices. Which method is MOST suitable?
+ Stratified random sampling || Each branch is a stratum sampled in proportion.
- Quota sampling || Not random.
- Cluster sampling of one branch || Ignores two branches.
- Census || Examines every invoice, which may not be needed.
explain: Stratified sampling ensures each group is represented in proportion.
mistake: Choosing cluster sampling.
tip: Represent each group fairly = stratified.
src: Study text ch2a - stratified sampling (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q026
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A population has 6,000 items. A systematic sample of 150 items is required. What sampling interval should be used?
answer: 40
tol: 0
unit: items
formula: Sampling interval = population size / sample size
step: 6,000 / 150 = 40.
step: Select a random start between 1 and 40, then every 40th item.
check: 6000/150
explain: The interval spreads the sample evenly through the list.
mistake: Dividing sample size by population.
tip: Interval = N / n.
src: Study text ch2a - systematic sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How can Big Data help businesses?
+ By revealing trends and customer behaviour to target resources and support strategy || Analysis supports faster, better-targeted decisions.
- By removing the need for any human judgement || Judgement is still needed.
- By guaranteeing every forecast is correct || No forecast is guaranteed.
- By replacing all financial records || Financial records are still required.
explain: Uses include creating business value, understanding customers and supporting corporate strategy.
mistake: Overstating what Big Data can do.
tip: Insight, not certainty.
src: Study text ch2a - Big Data uses (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What is the difference between sample data and population data?
+ Sample data comes from a selection of the population; population data comes from every member || Coverage differs.
- Sample data is always more accurate || Not necessarily.
- Population data is always secondary || Not necessarily.
- There is no difference || There is.
explain: Sampling saves time and cost but introduces sampling error.
mistake: Thinking a sample is the same as a census.
tip: Some vs all.
src: Study text ch2a - sample and population (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Why is quota sampling popular for market research?
+ It is cheap and simple, without needing a sampling frame || It is practical for street interviews.
- It gives every person a known chance of selection || It is non-probability.
- It eliminates interviewer bias || It can increase bias.
- It examines every member of the population || That is a census.
explain: Quota sampling trades statistical rigour for practicality.
mistake: Thinking quota sampling is unbiased.
tip: Cheap and quick, but not random.
src: Study text ch2a - quota sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which external source would MOST likely give detailed information about a specific industry's trends?
+ A trade journal || Trade journals focus on particular industries.
- The firm's payroll || Internal.
- The firm's cash book || Internal.
- The firm's production records || Internal.
explain: Trade journals, consultancies and information bureaux provide specialist secondary data.
mistake: Choosing an internal record.
tip: Industry trends = trade journals.
src: Study text ch2a - secondary sources (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Put the steps for selecting a systematic sample in order.
item: Obtain a numbered list of the population
item: Calculate the sampling interval (population / sample size)
item: Choose a random starting point within the first interval
item: Select every nth item from the starting point
explain: A sampling frame and interval are needed before the random start and selection.
mistake: Choosing the start before calculating the interval.
tip: List, interval, start, select.
src: Study text ch2a - systematic sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: S
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company uses last year's published industry report to estimate the size of its market. What are the advantage and risk of using this data?
+ It is cheap and quick (secondary), but it may be out of date or not fit the company's purpose exactly || Typical trade-off of secondary data.
- It is primary data, so it fits exactly || It is secondary.
- It is always more accurate than primary data || Not necessarily.
- It is free from any limitations || It has limitations.
explain: Secondary data saves cost but may not exactly match the current need.
mistake: Assuming published data is always suitable.
tip: Check the date and purpose of secondary data.
src: Study text ch2a - secondary data (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q033
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: How does multistage sampling make large surveys more practical?
+ It narrows the population in stages, so data collection is concentrated in selected areas || Fewer locations need to be visited.
- It surveys every member of the population || That is a census.
- It removes the need for any random selection || Random selection is used at each stage.
- It always gives a perfectly representative sample || Not guaranteed.
explain: Multistage sampling is often used for national surveys.
mistake: Thinking multistage removes randomness.
tip: Divide, select, then sample within.
src: Study text ch2a - multistage sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which BEST illustrates the "variety" dimension of Big Data?
+ Data from social media posts, videos, sensors and transaction records combined || Many formats.
- A very large spreadsheet of sales figures only || Volume, but little variety.
- Data processed in real time only || Velocity.
- Data that is always accurate || Not a V.
explain: Big Data includes structured and unstructured data in many formats.
mistake: Confusing variety with volume.
tip: Variety = many types.
src: Study text ch2a - three Vs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t4
status: review
important: 1
q: Which list contains ONLY probability sampling methods?
+ Simple random, stratified, systematic, multistage || Each has a known chance of selection.
- Quota, cluster, convenience, systematic || Quota and convenience are non-probability.
- Census, quota, stratified, random || A census is not a sampling method and quota is non-probability.
- Quota, simple random, multistage, stratified || Quota is non-probability.
explain: Quota sampling is the main non-probability method in the syllabus.
mistake: Including quota sampling.
tip: Quota = no known chance.
src: Study text ch2a - sampling methods (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A company has 1,200 employees: 720 in production, 360 in sales and 120 in administration. A stratified sample of 80 employees is required. How many SALES employees should be included?
answer: 24
tol: 0
unit: people
formula: Stratum sample = (stratum size / population) x sample size
step: Sales share = 360 / 1,200 = 30%.
step: Sales sample = 30% x 80 = 24.
step: Check: production 48, sales 24, administration 8 = 80.
check: 360/1200*80
explain: Stratified samples mirror the population's composition.
mistake: Splitting the sample equally (26.7 each).
tip: Check that the strata add back to the total sample.
src: Study text ch2a - stratified sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q037
type: mr
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
select: 2
q: A company wants to survey opinions of its 50,000 customers, who are spread across the country, but has a limited budget and no complete customer list. Which TWO methods are MOST practical?
+ Quota sampling || It needs no sampling frame and is cheap.
+ Cluster sampling of selected areas || It concentrates data collection.
- Simple random sampling from a full numbered list || No complete list exists.
- A census of all customers || Too costly.
explain: Practical constraints (cost, no frame, dispersion) often favour cluster or quota methods, accepting some loss of representativeness.
mistake: Choosing a method requiring a sampling frame.
tip: Check what resources each method needs.
src: Study text ch2a - choosing methods (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q038
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: An analysis of Big Data shows that stores selling more umbrellas also sell more hot drinks. A manager concludes that umbrellas cause hot drink sales. What is the flaw?
+ The two may simply be linked by a third factor, such as rainy cold weather - correlation does not prove causation || A classic Big Data criticism.
- Big Data cannot detect any relationships || It can detect correlations.
- The data must be wrong || The correlation may be real.
- Umbrellas must reduce hot drink sales || Not supported.
explain: Large datasets can reveal patterns, but managers must avoid assuming cause and effect.
mistake: Treating correlation as causation.
tip: Look for a hidden common cause.
src: Study text ch2a - Big Data criticisms (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: A sales list is ordered by day of the week, with seven entries per week. A trainee chooses every 7th entry for a systematic sample. What is the risk?
+ Every selected entry may fall on the same weekday, biasing the sample || The interval matches the pattern.
- The sample will be too large || Size is not the issue.
- It is not a probability method || It is, but it is biased here.
- There is no risk || A pattern creates bias.
explain: Systematic sampling is biased when the interval coincides with a pattern in the list.
mistake: Overlooking patterns in ordered data.
tip: Interval + pattern = bias.
src: Study text ch2a - systematic sampling (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q040
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t4
status: review
q: A researcher divides customers into age groups and lets interviewers choose who to interview until each group's target is met. A colleague calls this "stratified random sampling". Is this correct?
+ No - selection within groups is not random, so it is quota sampling || Interviewer choice makes it non-probability.
- Yes - any grouping by age is stratified random sampling || Random selection within groups is required.
- No - it is a census || Not every customer is interviewed.
- Yes - quota and stratified sampling are identical || They differ in how individuals are selected.
explain: Stratified and quota sampling both use groups; only stratified uses random selection within each group.
mistake: Confusing the two because both use groups.
tip: Groups + random = stratified; groups + interviewer choice = quota.
src: Study text ch2a - stratified vs quota (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A company has 15 regions. It randomly selects 3 regions, then 4 branches in each selected region, then 25 customers at each selected branch. How many customers are sampled?
answer: 300
tol: 0
unit: people
formula: Total = regions selected x branches per region x customers per branch
step: Branches selected = 3 x 4 = 12.
step: Customers = 12 x 25 = 300.
check: 3*4*25
explain: In multistage sampling, the total sample is the product of the numbers selected at each stage. The 15 regions in the population do not enter the calculation.
mistake: Multiplying by 15 regions instead of the 3 selected.
tip: Use only the numbers SELECTED at each stage.
src: Study text ch2a - multistage sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: match
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: Match each situation with the MOST appropriate sampling method.
pair: A full numbered list exists and every item should have an equal chance => Simple random
pair: The population has distinct groups that must each be represented in proportion => Stratified random
pair: A long numbered list with no pattern and a need for simplicity => Systematic
pair: A street survey with a small budget and no list of the population => Quota
choice: Census
explain: The choice depends on the available sampling frame, budget and the need for representativeness.
mistake: Choosing a census when sampling is adequate.
tip: Match method to resources and purpose.
src: Study text ch2a - choosing a method (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A manager wants to forecast next year's labour costs and uses both the firm's payroll records and government forecasts of wage inflation. Which statement is correct?
+ Payroll records are an internal source; government forecasts are an external secondary source || Combining sources improves the forecast.
- Both are internal sources || Government forecasts are external.
- Both are primary data || Neither was collected for this forecast.
- Payroll records are external || They are internal.
explain: Good forecasts often combine internal records with external economic data.
mistake: Calling government data primary.
tip: Inside records + outside forecasts.
src: Study text ch2a - sources (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: A survey records each customer's exact age in years and months. Later the results are grouped into "under 30", "30-50" and "over 50". What has happened to the data?
+ Continuous-type measurements have been grouped into categories, losing some detail || Grouping simplifies but loses precision.
- Discrete data has become continuous || Grouping does not create continuity.
- Primary data has become secondary || The purpose has not changed.
- Qualitative data has become quantitative || Age was already quantitative.
explain: Grouping data aids presentation but reduces the information available.
mistake: Thinking grouping changes the data source.
tip: Grouping = simpler but less precise.
src: Study text ch2a - types of data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: An online retailer analyses millions of website clicks in real time to change the products it recommends to each visitor. Which Big Data features are MOST evident?
+ High volume and high velocity || Millions of clicks processed in real time.
- Low volume and low velocity || The opposite is described.
- Only variety || Volume and speed are more evident.
- None - this is ordinary bookkeeping || It is Big Data analysis.
explain: Big Data enables real-time decisions such as personalised recommendations.
mistake: Overlooking velocity.
tip: Real time = velocity.
src: Study text ch2a - Big Data (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Why might a random sample still be UNREPRESENTATIVE?
+ By chance, it may include too many or too few items from particular groups || Randomness does not guarantee balance.
- Because random samples are always biased || Not always.
- Because random samples are too large || Size is not the issue.
- Because random samples never use a sampling frame || They do use one.
explain: Stratified sampling can reduce this risk by ensuring each group is represented.
mistake: Assuming random means representative.
tip: Random is fair, not guaranteed balanced.
src: Study text ch2a - random sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A stratified sample of 120 is drawn from 3,000 students: 1,800 undergraduates, 900 postgraduates and 300 research students. How many RESEARCH students should be sampled?
answer: 12
tol: 0
unit: people
formula: Stratum sample = (stratum size / population) x sample size
step: Research share = 300 / 3,000 = 10%.
step: 10% x 120 = 12.
check: 300/3000*120
explain: Each stratum's share of the sample equals its share of the population.
mistake: Using the share of undergraduates by mistake.
tip: Identify the correct stratum before calculating.
src: Study text ch2a - stratified sampling (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: A council surveys every household in one randomly chosen street to estimate opinions across the whole city. What is the main weakness?
+ One street may not represent the whole city, so results may be biased || Cluster sampling risks unrepresentativeness.
- It is too expensive compared with a census || A census of the city would cost more.
- It gives every household in the city a known chance of being surveyed in that street || The weakness is representativeness.
- It requires interviewer quotas || Not quota sampling.
explain: Cluster sampling is cheap and convenient but may not reflect the population.
mistake: Thinking any random choice ensures representativeness.
tip: One cluster may be unusual.
src: Study text ch2a - cluster sampling (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: When is a CENSUS more appropriate than a sample?
+ When the population is small and every item matters, such as auditing a few very large transactions || Complete coverage is feasible and valuable.
- When the population is huge and resources are limited || Sampling is better then.
- When speed is essential and the population is large || A census is slow.
- Never - samples are always better || Censuses are appropriate in some cases.
explain: Censuses avoid sampling error but are costly; samples are usually more practical for large populations.
mistake: Thinking censuses are never used.
tip: Small population + high importance = census.
src: Study text ch2a - census (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t1
status: review
important: 1
q: Which pairing is correct?
+ Discrete: countable values; continuous: any value within a range || This is the key distinction.
- Discrete: any value; continuous: countable values || Reversed.
- Primary: collected for another purpose; secondary: collected for this purpose || Reversed.
- Qualitative: measured; quantitative: described || Reversed.
explain: These classifications are basic but frequently tested.
mistake: Reversing definitions.
tip: Count = discrete; measure = continuous.
src: Study text ch2a - types of data (concept basis; original question)
